      "message": "Agreed, shared/weak ptr and their usages over vector\u003cvector\u003e and unordered sets can be quite slow. I liked the suggestion here as it\u0027d mainly slow down the snapshot saving time. I am not sure if there are any edge cases with it; even if we re-create the same buffer handle after a deletion, I don\u0027t think it\u0027d be invalid to still write that on into the descriptor set, since it\u0027s not expected to be used anyway.",
      "parentUuid": "43976d01_70bcfb11",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 566,
        "startChar": 24,
        "endLine": 568,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "4a4c038b93bb4b0a1ce090c447bc2a0ce54ab46e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6e392a82_accc370a",
        "filename": "host/vulkan/VkDecoderGlobalState.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 17
      },
      "lineNbr": 568,
      "author": {
        "id": 1087303
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-05-15T21:13:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The main concern was for reusing handles after deletion and recreation, because driver implementation might choose to reuse deleted handles. The current implementation is definitely the safest, but let me look up the spec to see if it says anything about deleted underlying objects (if users try to use it, should it be undefined behavior or should it give default values)",
      "parentUuid": "d6ba3587_6b07f3a2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 566,
        "startChar": 24,
        "endLine": 568,
        "endChar": 25
      },
      "revId": "4a4c038b93bb4b0a1ce090c447bc2a0ce54ab46e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4d35dc43_e9a2ef44",
        "filename": "host/vulkan/VkDecoderGlobalState.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 17
      },
      "lineNbr": 568,
      "author": {
        "id": 1561731
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-05-16T11:41:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ahh that\u0027s a good point and is exactly the issue we saw in aosp/2937022.\n\nI guess the question is: does it matter if we encounter a recycled handle in this case? With something like:\n\n```\nT1: create \"vkbuffer1\" (original)\nT2: update binding1 of vkdescriptorset1 with vkbuffer1\nT3: draw\nT4: delete \"vkbuffer1\" (original)\nT5: create \"vkbuffer1\" (recycled)\nT6: snapshot load\n\n...\n\nT99: snapshot load\n```\n\nIIUC, the guest already is not allowed to use binding1 from vkdescriptorset1 anymore based on https://registry.khronos.org/vulkan/specs/1.3-extensions/man/html/vkAllocateDescriptorSets.html\n\n\u003e Descriptors also become undefined if the underlying resource or view object is destroyed. Descriptor sets containing undefined descriptors can still be bound and used, subject to the following conditions: \u003ccouple conditions\u003e\n\nwhere the conditions say \"don\u0027t actually use\" the undefined ones.\n\nSo I guess the worry is just about the `vkUpdateDescriptorSets()` on the snapshot load. IIUC, if there is a recycled handle, that means that there is at least a valid buffer/image available... so potentially snapshot load can just go ahead and update the descriptor set to a different buffer/image since it will not be used anyways? Is there ever a case where one type of image cannot be used in place of another image?",
      "parentUuid": "6e392a82_accc370a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 566,
        "startChar": 24,
        "endLine": 568,
        "endChar": 25
